    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.26491/mhwm/217736",
    "abstract": "Understanding future temperature changes in Sudan is essential for climate-risk assessment and adaptation planning in this highly vulnerable and data-scarce region.This study evaluates robust projected changes in near-surface air temperature at both annual and seasonal scales in summer (June-September) and winter (December-February) relative to the historical baseline period (1995)(1996)(1997)(1998)(1999)(2000)(2001)(2002)(2003)(2004)(2005)(2006)(2007)(2008)(2009)(2010)(2011)(2012)(2013)(2014).Projections are provided for the mid-century (2041-2060) and late-century (2081-2100) under the moderate SSP2-4.5 and high-emission SSP5-8.5 scenarios, based on a bias-corrected ensemble of CMIP6 climate models.Bias correction was applied using Equidistant Quantile Mapping (EQM) for historical simulations and Delta Quantile Mapping (DQM) for future projections to ensure consistency with observed climatology.The results indicate pronounced, scenario-dependent warming across all seasons.By mid-century, annual mean temperatures are projected to rise by 1.68C under SSP2-4.5 and 2.17C under SSP5-8.5,reaching 2.68C and 4.87C, respectively, by late-century.Seasonal analysis reveals the most intense and variable warming during summer, while winter exhibits more stable increases with narrower inter-model uncertainties.Spatially, northern and central Sudan are expected to experience the strongest warming, with high inter-model agreement.These findings underscore escalating heat-stress risks and highlight the urgent need for emissions mitigation and targeted adaptation strategies to support climate-resilient planning and sustainable development in Sudan.",
    "field": "climate change",
    "problem": "Existing climate assessments for Sudan are often hindered by coarse spatial resolution, a scarcity of seasonal temperature projections, and inter-model uncertainty, which obscures critical local patterns needed for robust adaptation planning in this vulnerable, data-scarce region.",
    "method": "This study uses a bias-corrected ensemble of CMIP6 climate models, applying Equidistant and Delta Quantile Mapping, to project robust future annual and seasonal temperature changes over Sudan under moderate and high-emission scenarios.",
    "task": "The task is to evaluate projected changes in near-surface air temperature over Sudan for the mid- and late-21st century using a bias-corrected ensemble of CMIP6 climate models under moderate (SSP2-4.5) and high-emission (SSP5-8.5) scenarios.",
